        "reasoning_content": null,
        "text": "1. Model Identity\nThe system must ensure that the loaded model weights and configuration metadata remain immutable during inference to guarantee consistent output behavior. A failure mode occurs if the model file is corrupted or swapped during runtime, leading to undefined behavior or security vulnerabilities. Verification is achieved by computing and comparing SHA-256 hashes of the model artifacts against a known good baseline before initialization.\n\n2. Resource Admission\nThe service must enforce a strict upper bound on concurrent requests to prevent memory exhaustion and ensure predictable latency for all active clients. A failure mode arises when the admission control logic fails to reject excess requests, causing the system to crash under load. Verification is performed by simulating a burst of requests exceeding the configured limit and confirming that the excess requests receive immediate rejection responses.\n\n3. Streaming\nThe output stream must deliver tokens in chronological order without gaps or reordering to maintain semantic coherence for the consumer. A failure mode happens if the internal buffer overflows or the network socket closes prematurely, resulting in truncated or incomplete responses. Verification is conducted by sending a long-prompt request and asserting that the total number of received tokens matches the expected count and arrives in the correct sequence.\n\n4. Cancellation\nThe system must immediately halt computation and release associated resources upon receiving a client-side cancellation signal to avoid wasted cycles. A failure mode occurs if the cancellation handler is blocked by a non-interruptible operation, leaving the request hanging and consuming memory indefinitely. Verification is achieved by initiating a generation task and sending a cancellation request mid-stream, then confirming that the server process terminates the task and returns control within a defined timeout.\n\n5. Shutdown\nThe service must gracefully drain all active requests and close network listeners within a specified timeout period during system termination. A failure mode happens if the shutdown sequence ignores in-flight requests, causing data loss or leaving the process in a zombie state. Verification is performed by triggering a shutdown signal while multiple requests are active and asserting that all pending responses are either completed or cleanly aborted before the process exits.",
